<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greater Hamburg Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1937</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Groß-Hamburg-Gesetz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> Act was a Nazi German law of 1937 that redrew the boundaries of several northern German states. It enlarged Hamburg with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altona">Altona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wandsbek">Wandsbek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harburg-wilhelmsburg">Harburg-Wilhelmsburg</a>, ended the independence of the Free City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, and transferred Oldenburg's exclaves of Lübeck and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkenfeld">Birkenfeld</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, while giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmshaven">Wilhelmshaven</a> to Oldenburg. It took effect on 1 April 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greater-hamburg-act.jpg" alt="Greater Hamburg Act" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aeroid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gro%C3%9F-Hamburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
